.\" eictar(1) - the encrypted individually-compressed tar
.\" View it with: man ./doc/eictar.1
.TH EICTAR 1 "2026-10-04" "eictar" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
eictar \- archive files, each compressed and optionally encrypted on its own
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eictar
.B \-c
.B \-f
.I archive
.RI [ options ]
.IR path ...
.br
.B eictar
.BR \-r | \-u
.B \-f
.I archive
.RI [ options ]
.IR path ...
.br
.B eictar
.BR \-t | \-x | \-\-verify | \-\-diff
.B \-f
.I archive
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ pattern ...]
.br
.B eictar
.B \-\-delete
.B \-f
.I archive
.IR pattern ...
.br
.B eictar
.BR \-\-compact | \-\-change\-passphrase | \-\-repair | \-\-info
.B \-f
.I archive
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B eictar
.BR \-\-list\-codecs | \-\-show\-config | \-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B eictar
works like
.BR tar (1),
but it compresses each member separately, and it can encrypt each member
separately. An index at the end of the archive lists every member, so a
listing reads only the end of the file, and an extraction of one file reads
only that file.
.PP
The archive is one seekable file. It cannot be read from a pipe or written
to one.
.PP
Changes to an archive add a new generation after the old one. The old
generation stays intact until the new one is complete, so a crash does not
damage what the archive held. A deleted or replaced member stays in the file
as a tombstone until
.B \-\-compact
removes it.
.PP
Paths are stored relative. A leading
.B /
or
.B ..
is removed when a file is added, and the program says so one time. There is
no option to keep an absolute path. Extraction checks each path again, and
it never writes outside its destination, not even through a symbolic link.
.SH OPERATIONS
Give exactly one operation.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-create
Create a new archive. An existing file at the path is replaced only when the
new archive is complete. If the create fails, the old file stays as it was.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-append
Add paths to an archive. A path that is already in the archive is replaced;
.B \-\-on\-conflict
changes that.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Add only the paths whose copy in the archive is out of date.
.B \-\-update\-mode
selects the test.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-list
List the members. With
.BR \-v ,
give the long listing. With
.BR \-vv ,
give more columns and a line of totals.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-extract
Extract the members, into the current directory or into
.BR \-d .
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Mark the members that match each pattern as deleted. Each pattern must match
a member, or nothing changes.
.TP
.B \-\-compact
Write the archive again without its dead space: tombstones, and old indexes.
.TP
.B \-\-change\-passphrase
Seal the key of an encrypted archive under a new passphrase. The program asks
for the old passphrase, then two times for the new one. It writes the archive
again, as
.B \-\-compact
does. The content is copied, not encrypted again. The Argon2id parameters stay
as they are, except those that a
.B \-\-kdf\-*
option gives.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Decode every member and check every digest and every authentication tag,
without extraction. Data that several files share is decoded one time.
The members are decoded in parallel, with
.B \-j
workers, and reported in archive order.
Patterns and
.B \-R
select the members to check.
.TP
.B \-\-diff
Compare the members with the files on disk, in the current directory or in
.BR \-C .
Show each path that is not on disk, each path on disk that is not in the
archive, and each difference of type, size, content, link target, mode,
owner, modification time, device numbers, extended attributes and ACLs.
Under a directory that is only on one side, only the directory is shown.
The content check reads each file of the same size, with
.B \-j
workers, and compares its digest with the member's digest. It reads no
member data, so it does not check the archive itself; use
.B \-\-verify
for that. Nothing is changed. The exit status is 1 if there is a
difference.
.IP
Paths on disk that are not in the archive are found in the directories that
the archive holds. Give the same
.BR \-\-exclude ,
.BR \-h ,
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
and
.B \-\-no\-xattrs
options as for the create, or what they changed shows as differences.
Patterns and
.B \-R
select the members, and the paths on disk, to compare. A file extracted
without
.B \-p
has its mode less the umask, and that is a difference.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
After a crash during a change, cut the file back to the last complete
generation. An archive that opens correctly is not changed.
.TP
.B \-\-info
Show the header, the encryption settings, the generation, the member counts,
the codecs, the dictionaries, the files that share another's data, and the
dead space.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-codecs
Show each codec with its parameters, defaults and ranges.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Archive and paths
.TP
.BI \-f " archive" "\fR, \fP\-\-file " archive
The archive. It is necessary for every operation except
.B \-\-list\-codecs
and
.BR \-\-show\-config .
The conventional extension is
.BR .ect .
.B \-c
adds it to a name with no extension, and says so: \fB\-cf backup\fP makes
.BR backup.ect .
The other operations then find
.B backup.ect
by the name
.BR backup .
A name with an extension, such as
.BR backup.tar ,
stays as it is.
.TP
.BI \-d " dir" "\fR, \fP\-\-destination " dir
Extract into
.IR dir ,
and create it if it does not exist.
.TP
.BI \-C " dir" "\fR, \fP\-\-directory " dir
Change to
.I dir
first.
.I dir
must exist.
.TP
.BI \-T " file" "\fR, \fP\-\-files\-from " file
Read the paths from
.IR file ,
or from standard input if
.I file
is
.BR \- .
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " glob"
Leave out the paths that match
.IR glob .
An excluded directory is not entered, and it takes its contents. It can be
given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-X " file" "\fR, \fP\-\-exclude\-from " file
Read one glob from each line of
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-R " re" "\fR, \fP\-\-regex " re
Keep only the paths that the regular expression
.I re
matches in full (see PATTERNS). With
.BR \-c ,
.B \-r
and
.BR \-u ,
it filters what the walk finds. With
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-x ,
.B \-\-verify
and
.BR \-\-delete ,
it filters the members. With
.BR \-\-diff ,
it filters both. It can be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-regex " re"
Leave out the paths that
.I re
matches in full. An excluded directory is not entered, and it takes its
contents. It can be given more than once.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-dereference
Store what a symbolic link points to, not the link. With
.BR \-\-diff ,
compare what it points to. For
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-r ,
.B \-u
and
.B \-\-diff
only.
.TP
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
Do not enter other filesystems. A mount point is stored, empty.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dedup
Store each copy of the same content in full. By default, a file whose
content the archive already holds shares that data, and stores nothing of
its own. Damage to shared data affects each file that shares it. This
option keeps damage inside one file, at the cost of space. Only
.BR \-c ,
.B \-r
and
.B \-u
take it.
.SS Compression
.TP
.BI \-Z " spec" "\fR, \fP\-\-compress " spec
Compress with
.IR spec :
.B none
or
.IR name [: key [= value ][, key [= value ]]...].
A
.I key
alone means its \(lqon\(rq value, the same as
.IB key =on\fR,\fP
and
.IB key =off
turns it off. The default is
.BR zstd ,
at level 12.
All the members that one operation adds use the same codec. An archive can
hold members from different codecs.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-gzip
The same as
.BR "\-\-compress gzip" .
.TP
.BR \-J ", " \-\-xz
The same as
.BR "\-\-compress xz" .
.TP
.B \-\-zstd
The same as
.BR "\-\-compress zstd" .
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk\-size " size"
Compress in chunks of
.I size
bytes, from 512 B to 256 MiB. The default is 4 MiB. A size can have a unit:
KiB, MiB or GiB.
.SS Encryption
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-encrypt
Encrypt the new archive. The program asks for the passphrase two times. Only
.B \-c
takes this option: every later operation uses the passphrase of the archive,
until
.B \-\-change\-passphrase
changes it.
.TP
.B \-\-encrypt\-index
Also encrypt the index, which hides the names, sizes and digests.
.TP
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " file"
Read the passphrase from the first line of
.IR file .
If the archive is not encrypted, the program refuses it with exit 3.
.TP
.BI \-\-passphrase\-env " var"
Read the passphrase from the environment variable
.IR var .
Other processes can see the environment, so the program prints a warning.
.TP
.BI \-\-new\-passphrase\-file " file" "\fR, \fP\-\-new\-passphrase\-env " var
With
.BR \-\-change\-passphrase ,
read the new passphrase from the first line of
.IR file ,
or from the environment variable
.IR var .
Without them, the program asks for it.
.TP
.BI \-\-kdf\-time " n" "\fR, \fP\-\-kdf\-memory " kib "\fR, \fP\-\-kdf\-threads " n
The Argon2id cost: passes (1 to 64, default 3), memory in KiB (at most
4194304, default 262144), and threads (default 4). Only
.B \-c
and
.B \-\-change\-passphrase
take these options.
.SS Changes
.TP
.BI \-\-on\-conflict " mode"
With
.BR \-r :
what to do with a path that is already in the archive.
.B replace
(the default),
.BR skip ,
or
.BR error ,
which stops and changes nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-update\-mode " mode"
With
.BR \-u :
.B newer
(the default) takes a file whose modification time is later.
.B different
takes a file whose time or size is different, in either direction.
.B digest
reads each file and takes it if its content is different.
.TP
.BI \-\-recompress " spec"
With
.BR \-\-compact :
encode every member again with
.IR spec .
.TP
.B \-\-quick
With
.BR \-\-verify :
check only the structure, and read no member data.
.SS Extraction
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-existing
Never replace an existing file.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Replace existing files. This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-newer\-only
Replace an existing file only when the member is newer.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-to\-stdout
Write the content to standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-strip\-components " N"
Remove the first
.I N
components from the path of each member, as tar does. For example,
.B \-\-strip\-components 1
extracts
.B project\-1.2/src/main.go
as
.BR src/main.go .
A member with
.I N
components or fewer is not extracted. The patterns,
.BR \-R ,
.B \-\-exclude
and
.B \-\-exclude\-regex
match the paths in the archive, before the strip. A path after the strip
gets the same checks as any other path.
.IP
When two members get the same path, the later member in path order is
extracted, and the program gives a warning that names both. The run then
ends with exit 1. Two directories with the same path are not a collision:
the contents of both go into one directory. A hard link to a member that is
not extracted gets a copy of its content.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-diff ,
compare the tree that extraction with this option writes. With
.BR \-n ,
show the paths after the strip. Not with
.BR \-O ,
which writes no paths.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve\-permissions
Restore the modes exactly: with the setuid, setgid and sticky bits, and not
less the umask. Also restore POSIX ACLs as any user, and, as root, the
privileged extended attributes
.RB ( security.* ,
.BR trusted.* ,
.BR system.* ).
.B security.capability
gives a program privilege, as setuid does, so give
.B \-p
only for an archive that you trust. Without
.BR \-p ,
a user who is not root gets each mode less the umask, and ACLs are
restored only as root.
.TP
.B \-\-preserve\-owner
Restore the owner. Only root can do this.
.TP
.B \-\-preserve\-devices
Create device nodes. Only root can do this.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-xattrs ", " \-\-no\-acls ", " \-\-no\-owner
When adding, do not record this metadata. When extracting, do not restore
it. With
.BR \-\-diff ,
do not compare it.
.SS Output and work
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show each member as it is processed. With
.BR \-t ,
see
.BR LISTING .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Show errors only.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
With
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-u ,
.B \-x
or
.BR \-\-delete :
show the paths that the operation would write or delete, and change
nothing. The operation selects the paths with the same code and the same
options as a real run:
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-\-exclude ,
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-\-on\-conflict ,
.BR \-\-update\-mode ,
.B \-k
and
.B \-\-newer\-only
all apply. Each path is on its own line on standard output, also with
.BR \-q .
With
.BR \-v ,
a word before each path tells what happens to it:
.BR add ,
.BR replace ,
.B extract
or
.BR delete .
A line on standard error gives the number of paths and the size of their
content.
.IP
A dry run makes no archive, locks no archive, and makes no destination
directory. It does not read the content of files, except for
.BR "\-u \-\-update\-mode digest" ,
which compares digests. Thus a file that cannot be read fails only in the
real run. A dry run of an encrypted archive asks for the passphrase when
it reads the archive: for
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-u ,
.B \-x
and
.BR \-\-delete ,
but not for
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-\-long
With
.BR \-t :
the same as
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B \-\-json
With
.BR \-t :
a JSON listing with every field, for programs.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Show a progress line on standard error, when it is a terminal.
.TP
.BI \-j " n" "\fR, \fP\-\-workers " n
Use
.I n
workers, from 1 to 1024. The default is three quarters of the CPUs,
rounded down, and at least 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-memory\-limit " size"
The memory for data in flight. The default comes from the size of the
machine.
.TP
.BI \-\-spill\-threshold " size"
Above this size, a member waits on disk, not in memory. The default is
32 MiB.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-going
Continue after an error on one member, and exit with 1 at the end.
When an archive is written, a directory that cannot be read is archived
empty, and a file that went away during the walk is left out. On macOS such
a directory is left out too: its extended attributes cannot be read.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read the configuration from
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Read no configuration file and no
.B EICTAR_*
variable.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-config
Show each setting, its value and where the value came from, then exit.
.SH LISTING
.B \-tv
gives one line for each member:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\-  alice/staff  240001  1432  99.4%  zstd:level=12  2026\-09\-24 17:59:19  d/text.txt
.RE
.fi
.PP
The columns are the type and mode, the owner, the size, the stored size, the
percentage that compression saved, the codec, the modification time and the
path. A member with no data of its own shows
.B \-
for the stored size, the percentage and the codec. A hardlink shares the
data of its target. A file with the same content as another shares its data
too, and the path ends with
.BI "same as " path\fR,\fP
.BR "same as its earlier version" ,
or
.BI "same as deleted " path\fR.\fP
The percentage can be less than zero. For example, each chunk in an
encrypted archive carries 16 bytes of authentication tag, which is more
than a tiny file.
.PP
.B \-tvv
adds the chunk count,
.B sealed
for an encrypted member, and the start of the BLAKE3 digest. A last line
gives the totals, and the files that share another's data.
.SH PATTERNS
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-\-verify ,
.BR \-\-diff ,
.B \-\-delete
and
.B \-\-exclude
take patterns. A pattern matches a member when it is the member's path, or
a directory that the member is under, or a glob that matches the whole path
(a
.B *
does not match
.BR / ).
A pattern with no
.B /
also matches any name in the path, at any depth: 
.B *.go
takes
.BR src/main.go ,
and
.B cache
takes
.B a/cache
and everything under it. A glob with a
.B /
matches the whole path only:
.B \(aqsrc/sub*\(aq
takes
.BR src/sub ,
but not
.BR src/sub/main.go .
An
.B \-\-exclude
pattern is the exception: a directory that it matches always takes its
contents, as the walk does not enter it. Each pattern, except an
.BR \-\-exclude ,
must match at least one member.
.PP
.B \-R
and
.B \-\-exclude\-regex
take regular expressions in the RE2 syntax of Go. An expression must match
the whole stored path: the path in the archive, relative, with
.B /
between the names. Thus
.B \(aq.*/dir/[a\-f][0\-9]+\e.txt\(aq
matches
.B x/dir/a1.txt
but not
.BR x/dir/a1.txt.bak .
In an expression,
.B .
is any character: write
.B \e.
for a dot, and put the expression in single quotes for the shell. Unlike a
pattern, an expression that matches a directory does not take its contents:
write
.B \(aqbuild(/.*)?\(aq
for that. Each
.B \-R
must match at least one path or member.
.PP
To see what a pattern or an expression selects before anything changes,
add
.B \-n
(see
.BR \-\-dry\-run ).
.PP
Put paths and patterns that start with
.B \-
after
.BR \-\- .
.SH CODECS
.TP
.B zstd
Zstandard. Parameters:
.B level
1 to 22 (default 12);
.B long
10 to 30, the window size as a power of 2, or alone for 27, as with
.BR "zstd \-\-long" ;
and
.BR train .
The chunks are independent, so a window larger than
.B \-\-chunk\-size
has no effect, and the program warns.
.IP
The zstd library of eictar has four speeds, not 22 levels: levels 1 and 2
are the fastest, 3 to 5 the default speed, 6 to 9 better, and 10 to 22 the
best. Thus
.B level=12
and
.B level=19
make the same archive. The default, 12, is the best speed: about 5% smaller
than level 3, and about 3 times slower to create. Extraction is as fast at
every level. The best speed holds about 44 MB of memory for each worker that
compresses at the same time. The program runs no more of them than fit in a
sixteenth of the RAM, or in half of
.BR \-\-memory\-limit .
.IP
.B train
makes a dictionary from the start of each file before the compression, and
stores it in the archive. Many small, similar files, such as a source tree
or JSON and YAML files, then compress better, and each file can still be
extracted alone.
.B train
alone, or
.BR train=auto ,
measures the best dictionary size on the files. The program trains
dictionaries of 4 KiB, 8 KiB, and so on up to 1 MiB, on three quarters of
the files. It compresses the other files with each dictionary, and it uses
the size that makes the smallest archive. When no dictionary saves more than
its own size, the program compresses without one, and says so. Two sizes
train at the same time, and no more than
.BR \-j .
.B train=SIZE
sets the size, 4 KiB to 1 MiB, and does not measure.
.B \-\-info
shows the size of the dictionary.
.IP
Use
.B train
for many small, similar files, such as JSON, YAML, Markdown or source code.
On such trees, it made the archive 5% to 40% smaller. For large files, or
for files that are already compressed, it saves almost nothing. Training
costs time: 120 source files took 2 s instead of 0.2 s, and a tree of
232 MB took 14 s instead of 1 s, for a gain of 0.4%. The measurement takes
1 to 2 times as long as a fixed size. With
.B train
alone, the program uses a dictionary only when the measurement shows a gain.
A fixed size can make the archive of a small tree larger.
.IP
With
.B \-r
and
.BR \-u ,
.B train
uses the dictionary that the archive already has.
.B "\-\-recompress zstd:train"
makes a new one from the whole archive. In an encrypted archive, the
dictionary is encrypted too. When there is too little to learn from, the
program compresses without a dictionary, and says so.
.TP
.BR gzip ", " flate
DEFLATE, in a gzip member or raw. Parameter:
.B level
1 to 9 (default 6).
.TP
.B xz
LZMA2, as in
.BR xz (1),
stored without the .xz container. Parameter:
.B preset
0 to 9 (default 6). It is slower than the C
.BR xz ,
and every preset uses the faster match finder. A preset changes only the
dictionary size, and a dictionary larger than
.B \-\-chunk\-size
has no effect. Thus at the default chunk size of 4 MiB, presets 3 to 9
compress the same, and the program warns when a preset that is given has a
larger dictionary. On source code, zstd at its default level makes a smaller
archive, faster.
.TP
.B s2
S2, of the Snappy family: very fast, with a lower ratio. Parameter:
.B mode
fast, better (default) or best.
.TP
.B none
No compression.
.PP
A chunk that compression makes larger is stored as it is. Examples:
.BR "\-\-compress zstd:level=19,long=27" ,
.BR "\-\-compress zstd:level=19,train" ,
.BR "\-\-compress xz:preset=9" .
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings come from these places. The first has priority:
.IP 1. 4
the command line
.IP 2. 4
.B EICTAR_*
environment variables
.IP 3. 4
the configuration file
.IP 4. 4
the built-in defaults
.PP
The configuration file is the first of:
.BR \-\-config ,
.BR $EICTAR_CONFIG ,
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/eictar/config
(or
.BR ~/.config/eictar/config ),
and
.BR ~/.eictarrc .
A file in the current directory is never read. The program refuses a
configuration file that another user owns, or that the group or other users
can write.
.PP
Each line is
.IB key " = " value\fR,\fP
where the key is a long option without its dashes. A
.B #
at the start of a line, or after a space, starts a comment.
.B [codec.\fIname\fP]
sections give default parameters for one codec:
.PP
.nf
.RS
compress   = zstd
workers    = 8
exclude    = *.o
exclude    = .cache

[codec.zstd]
level = 12
train = on
.RE
.fi
.PP
A variable is
.B EICTAR_
and the long option in capitals, with
.B _
for
.BR \- ,
for example
.B EICTAR_CHUNK_SIZE=8MiB
and
.BR EICTAR_CODEC_ZSTD_LEVEL=19 .
.PP
A configured value applies only to the operations that use it. The
operation, the archive, the paths and the passphrase never come from a
configuration. An unknown key or variable is an error, so that a spelling
mistake is not ignored.
.B \-v
names the configuration file that was read. Scripts can use
.B \-\-no\-config
so that only the command line decides.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation completed, but some members failed under
.BR \-\-keep\-going .
Also, for
.BR \-\-diff ,
the archive and the disk are different. Also, with
.BR \-\-strip\-components ,
two members had the same path, and one was left out.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: a bad option, a bad configuration, a pattern or an
.B \-R
that matches nothing, a regular expression that is not valid, or a conflict
under
.BR \-\-on\-conflict=error .
Nothing was changed.
.TP
.B 3
The archive failed a check: damaged data, a wrong passphrase, a failed
authentication tag, an unsafe path, or a plain archive where a passphrase
was given. Also an archive path through a symbolic link that the program
does not follow.
.TP
.B 4
Any other error, for example an I/O error, or another eictar that is
changing the archive.
.TP
.B 70
An option that this build does not support yet.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EICTAR_CONFIG
The configuration file.
.TP
.BI EICTAR_ option
A setting; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " HOME
Where the configuration file is.
.SH SECURITY
.IP \(bu 2
eictar asks for the passphrase for every operation on an encrypted archive,
a listing too. But without
.BR \-\-encrypt\-index ,
the index is readable with other tools: names, sizes, times and owners. In an encrypted
archive, the digest of each file is a keyed BLAKE3 hash. Thus a person who
has a copy of a file cannot find out from the digests if the archive holds
it. Equal digests, and files that share data, still show which files have
the same content.
.IP \(bu 2
An encrypted archive detects changes to its content and to its index. A
plain archive detects damage, but not a person who writes the file again.
To know that an archive is the one that you made, keep its hash in a safe
place.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-O ,
the content goes to standard output while it is decoded. For a damaged
member of a plain archive, some bytes can come out before the digest check
fails. The exit status says so.
.IP \(bu 2
Two eictar processes cannot change one archive at the same time: the second
stops with exit 4. The lock is advisory, and it is not reliable on NFS.
.IP \(bu 2
Extraction never changes the destination directory itself, even for an
archive that holds an entry for its root
.RB ( . ).
.IP \(bu 2
When root archives a tree that other users can write, a file or a directory
that becomes a symbolic link during the walk is not followed. It fails, with
a message. The
archive path given with
.B \-f
follows a symbolic link in a directory such as
.I /tmp
only when the link belongs to the user or to the owner of the directory, as
the Linux kernel does. Otherwise the program stops with exit 3.
.IP \(bu 2
Identical content and
.B train
work across files. Thus a person who can add files to a tree that is
archived again and again, and who can see the size of the archive, can test
if the archive holds a guessed file. For such a tree, use
.B \-\-no\-dedup
and no
.BR train .
.IP \(bu 2
On a terminal, eictar escapes each character of a name that a terminal can
act on: a control character shows as
.BR \(rsn ,
.BR \(rst ,
.B \(rsr
or
.BR \(rsxHH ,
a byte that is not UTF\-8 as
.BR \(rsxHH ,
a C1 control character or a character that changes the direction of the
text as
.BR \(rsuHHHH ,
and a backslash as
.BR \(rs\(rs .
Thus a crafted name cannot change what the terminal shows. To a pipe or a
file, names are exact; use
.B \-\-json
to read any name safely. Messages on standard error are always escaped.
.IP \(bu 2
A passphrase file that other users can read gives a warning.
.IP \(bu 2
Every key comes from a random data key, which the passphrase seals.
.B \-\-change\-passphrase
seals the same data key under a new passphrase. The blocks of the old file
can stay on the disk. Thus a person with a copy of the old file, or with the
old passphrase and the disk, can still read what the archive held then.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-update\-mode=newer
misses a file that a restore from backup made older. Use
.B different
for backups.
.SH PLATFORMS
Linux has all the metadata. macOS, FreeBSD, NetBSD and OpenBSD have the
owner, the special bits, the times, hardlinks and symbolic links, with these
limits:
.IP \(bu 2
macOS records no ACLs, and extraction skips named pipes and device nodes
with a notice. APFS refuses a file name that is not UTF-8.
.IP \(bu 2
NetBSD and OpenBSD store a file with holes dense.
.IP \(bu 2
OpenBSD has no extended attributes.
.PP
Extended attribute names are stored as the source platform gives them:
.B user.comment
on Linux,
.B com.apple.quarantine
on macOS. Extraction sets each name that the destination takes, and one
notice lists the others. On other platforms, eictar stores content,
directories and links only.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create an encrypted archive of ~/Documents, stored as
.BR Documents/... ,
with zstd at the default level 12:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-cef home.ect \-C ~ Documents
.RE
.fi

.PP
Add what changed since then, and check the result:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-uf home.ect \-C ~ \-\-update\-mode different Documents
eictar \-\-verify \-f home.ect
.RE
.fi
.PP
Show what changed in Documents since the last update:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-\-diff \-f home.ect \-C ~
.RE
.fi
.PP
Extract one directory into another place:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-xf home.ect \-d /tmp/restore Documents/letters
.RE
.fi
.PP
Extract a release without its top directory, and check the result:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-xf project\-1.2.ect \-d project \-\-strip\-components 1
eictar \-\-diff \-f project\-1.2.ect \-C project \-\-strip\-components 1
.RE
.fi
.PP
Remove old versions, and encode everything again with xz:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-\-compact \-f home.ect \-\-recompress xz:preset=9
.RE
.fi
.PP
Change the passphrase, and make the key derivation stronger:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-\-change\-passphrase \-f home.ect \-\-kdf\-memory 1048576
.RE
.fi
.PP
Extract only the PDF files below Documents, at any depth:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-xf home.ect \-R 'Documents/.*\e.pdf'
.RE
.fi
.PP
See which files an expression picks up, before the archive is made:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-cnf src.ect \-R '.*\e.go' project
.RE
.fi
.PP
Archive a source tree fast, with a dictionary, and without the build
directories:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eictar \-cf src.ect \-Z zstd:level=3,train \-\-exclude\-regex '.*/build' project
.RE
.fi
